{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774742,"nonce":"vYgaL0+zbN38AQsUPynQKSl6LXM=","title":"fix(ci): repair the nightly e2e job and de-flake the lease contract under coverage","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #88 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `fix/ci-e2e-toolcache-and-lease-flake` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-26T07:27:06Z · Merged: 2026-07-26T08:39:19Z\n\nTwo independent CI failures, two independent causes. Neither was a product bug.\n\n\nThe nightly job has **never had a green run**. It died ~2 minutes in, before a\nsingle test:\n\n```\nCache directory '/opt/hostedtoolcache' does not exist\n```\n\n`$AGENT_TOOLSDIRECTORY` *is* `/opt/hostedtoolcache`. The \"Free runner disk space\"\nstep `rm -rf`s it, eleven lines above the `helm/kind-action` step that installs\nkind *through* that cache.\n\nThe comparison makes it conclusive:\n\n| workflow | deletes `$AGENT_TOOLSDIRECTORY`? | uses the tool cache? | result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `kind.yml:66` | no | yes (`kind-action`) | passes |\n| `ci.yml:45,85` | yes | no (only `test`/`coverage`) | passes |\n| `e2e.yml:28` | **yes** | **yes** | fails every run |\n\nFixed by recreating the emptied directory (owned by the runner) rather than\ndropping it from the `rm` — that keeps both the ~8 GB the workspace build needs\nand the kind install. Guarded on the variable being non-empty so an unset\n`AGENT_TOOLSDIRECTORY` can't fail the step under `bash -e`.\n\n\n```\ndb_contract.rs:270: a renewed lease must be EXTENDED past the original expiry\n  left: \"b\"   right: \"a\"\n```\n\nEvidence it was the test, not the lease code:\n\n- the sibling `test` job — same suite, same Postgres, **no** instrumentation —\n  passed in the same run\n- a re-run of the identical commit went green\n- it hit two unrelated branches within 10 minutes while `main` passed\n\nThe assertion only holds if the probe lands within `ttl` of the *renewal*. At\n`ttl = 800ms`, renewing at +500ms and probing +500ms later left **300 ms** of\nslack — and the failing run's own log shows the test spending **539 ms** beyond\nits 1600 ms of sleeps under `cargo llvm-cov`. Past that, the lease really has\nexpired, `b` really does win, and a stalled runner impersonates a semantic bug.\n\nTwo changes:\n\n- **Absolute deadlines.** A chain of `sleep(d)` folds every earlier step's\n  overhead into the gap that matters. With `sleep_until` off one start instant,\n  that gap is `0.75·ttl` plus the *difference* of two single-call latencies.\n- **Fractional margins** (`ttl` 800 → 2000 ms), so slack scales with the budget\n  instead of being a fixed few hundred ms.\n\nPlus the precondition is now asserted rather than assumed: the gap since renewal\nis sampled *after* the probe returns (so it over-counts) and checked against the\nTTL before the ownership assertion. A genuinely stalled runner now reports \"the\nrunner stalled\" instead of a false lease-semantics failure.\n\n\n- Full `db_contract` suite under `cargo llvm-cov` against real Postgres: **12/12\n  pass**, with `lease_semantics::postgres` absorbing **791 ms** of overhead where\n  the old design's entire budget was 300 ms. Costs ~2.4 s of wall clock.\n- `e2e.yml` YAML parses and the embedded script passes `bash -n`.\n\n**Honest limitation:** I could not reproduce the lease failure locally — the old\nversion passed 8/8 even under full CPU saturation (10 cores). This machine is\nfaster than a 4-core runner with a service container and 110 test binaries in\nflight; local timing (2.147 s) was near-identical to CI's failing run (2.139 s),\nso what differed was *where* the overhead landed, not how much. The fix rests on\nthe arithmetic above plus CI evidence, not on a local red-to-green.\n\nThe e2e fix can only be proven by running the workflow — dispatched against this\nbranch.\n\n\nThe 2026-07-25 `coverage` failures were a *different* cause — the ratchet step\nrejecting `scarab-forge-github` (63.0% vs 65.8%) and `scarab-forge` (94.6% vs\n95.3%) after ADR-0060 part E landed `preflight.rs` untested. Already fixed on\nmain by `243bf3f`, with the baseline untouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n---\n\n\n**@thulasi-ram** — 2026-07-26T07:48:00Z\n\n\n**`e2e` — the first green run this workflow has ever had** ([30192830353](https://github.com/thulasi-ram/scarab-ci/actions/runs/30192830353)), dispatched against this branch:\n\n```\nsuccess  Free runner disk space\nsuccess  Run helm/kind-action@v1     \u003c- the step that aborted every prior run\nsuccess  Run the full-stack E2E suite\n```\n\nFor contrast, the same workflow on `main`: `2026-07-26 05:43 failure`, `2026-07-25 05:26 failure`.\n\nThe suite itself passed too, so the tool-cache deletion was the *only* thing wrong with the job — no further breakage was hiding behind it.\n\n**`ci` on this branch — green, including the two jobs at issue** ([30192826121](https://github.com/thulasi-ram/scarab-ci/actions/runs/30192826121)):\n\n```\ntest: success\ncoverage: success\n```\n\nAll three workflows on the branch (`ci`, `e2e`, `image`) are green.\n\nOne caveat worth keeping: `coverage` going green here is consistent with the fix but is not by itself proof, since the flake was intermittent — a single green run is what the *unfixed* branch also produced on re-run. The load-bearing evidence is the arithmetic in the PR body plus the local `llvm-cov` run absorbing 791 ms of overhead against the old design's 300 ms budget.","files":null}]}